## Customs Duty Calculator

Indonesia uses the **CIF (Cost + Insurance + Freight)** value for duty calculation:

| Device Value (USD) | Import Duty | VAT (PPN) | Estimated Total Tax |
|---|---|---|---|
| Under USD 75 | 0% | 0% | Free |
| USD 75 – 500 | 10% + BM | 11% | ~20–30% of value |
| Over USD 500 | 20% + BM | 11% | ~35–45% of value |
| Laptop/tablets | 0% BM | 11% | ~11% of value |

*BM = Import Duty rate varies by HS code. Check bea cukai.go.id for the latest rates.*

## Buying a Phone in Indonesia: Second-Hand Safety Tips

Markets in Jakarta's ITC Roxy Mas, Surabaya's WTC, or listings on OLX Indonesia carry risk of unregistered devices. Before buying:

1. Ask the seller to dial `*#06#`
2. Check the IMEI at **imei.kemenperin.go.id**
3. Run a [WorldWide Blacklist Check](https://imeihub.net/service.php?slug=blacklist) for international stolen status
4. Verify the IMEI matches what's printed on the box

## Frequently Asked Questions

**Does IMEI registration mean I own the phone legally?**
Registration confirms the device is legally imported into Indonesia. It does not resolve other ownership disputes.

**Can tourists use their phones without registering?**
Yes. Tourists are exempt for up to 90 days. However, if you stay longer or establish Indonesian residency, registration is required.

**I ordered a phone from abroad and it wasn't registered at customs — what now?**
Contact Bea Cukai at beacukai.go.id/informasi-kepabeanan or call the hotline at 1500225. You may be able to post-register with penalty fees.
